## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to design a privacy-conscious, scalable fraud-detection pipeline across multiple tenants with governance and cost controls.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation
- Privacy-preserving aggregation (secure sum, differential privacy)
- Vertex AI Pipelines and canary rollout
- Dataflow enrichment, BigQuery provenance
- Data Catalog policy tagging
- Drift detection and explainability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo example: privacy budget check
function shouldRollout(latencyMs, drift, budget) {
  const okLatency = latencyMs < 250;
  const okDrift = drift < 0.1;
  const okBudget = budget.remaining >= budget.required;
  return okLatency && okDrift && okBudget;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement per-tenant feature stores in Vertex AI Feature Store?
- How would you simulate a canary rollout with rollbacks if fraud latency spikes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests multi-tenant design under privacy, governance, and ops constraints typical at large platforms, plus data residency and cost visibility.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant feature stores and private endpoints for isolation
- Privacy-preserving cross-tenant aggregation (secure aggregation + DP-SGD)
- Canary rollout and multi-region deployment for safe updates
- Data provenance with BigQuery Data Catalog and lineage tracking
- Per-tenant cost controls and explicit rollback criteria
- Data residency considerations (EU vs US tenants)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: privacy-preserving cross-tenant gradient aggregation
function aggregateCrossTenantGradients(grads, epsilon, delta) {
  // clip per-example gradients, add Gaussian noise, sum and normalize
  const clipped = grads.map(g => clip(g, 1.0));
  const sum = clipped.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  const noise = gaussianNoise(epsilon, delta, sum.length);
  return (sum + noise) / grads.length;
}
```

